<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a null pointer dereference of the global variable `policydbp` inside the `define_policy` function.

The fuzzer `LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput` creates a `sepol_policydb_t` object named `policydb` and passes it as an argument to `yyparse`. However, the parser functions, including `define_policy`, operate on a global `policydb_t` pointer named `policydbp`. The fuzzer fails to initialize this global pointer with the address of the `policydb` object it created.

To fix this, the fuzzer must explicitly set the global `policydbp` before calling `yyparse`. This requires declaring `policydbp` as an external variable in the fuzzer and assigning the created `policydb` to it.

**Suggestion:**

Add the following lines to `checkpolicy-fuzzer.c` before the call to `yyparse`:

```c
// In checkpolicy-fuzzer.c

// Add this declaration at the top with other externs
extern policydb_t *policydbp;

...

int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
...
sepol_policydb_t *policydb = NULL;
if (sepol_policydb_create(&policydb) != 0) {
...
}

// Set the global policydbp used by the parser
policydbp = policydb; // This is not a typo, it's assigning the local to the global. A better name for the local would avoid confusion.

YY_BUFFER_STATE buf = yy_scan_string(null_terminated_data);

if (yyparse(handle, policydb) == 0) {
...
}
```
A better implementation would be to rename the local variable to avoid confusion:
```c
// In checkpolicy-fuzzer.c

// Add this declaration at the top with other externs
extern policydb_t *policydbp;

...

int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
...
sepol_policydb_t *local_policydb = NULL;
if (sepol_policydb_create(&local_policydb) != 0) {
...
}

// Set the global policydbp used by the parser
policydbp = local_policydb;

YY_BUFFER_STATE buf = yy_scan_string(null_terminated_data);

if (yyparse(handle, local_policydb) == 0) {
...
}
```
This change ensures that the parser functions operate on the correctly initialized policy database, preventing the null pointer dereference.
</crash-analysis>

